  "Question": "The first three terms of an arithmetic progression are \\(x-1,\\) \\(x+1,\\) \\(2x+3,\\) in the order shown. The value of \\(x\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-2\\)",
    "B": "\\(0\\)",
    "C": "\\(2\\)",
    "D": "\\(4\\)",
    "E": "undetermined"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "An arithmetic progression adds the same number at every step. The difference between the first two terms is\n\\[\n(x+1)-(x-1)=2.\n\\]\nThe third term must therefore be \\((x+1)+2=x+3\\). Equating this with the given third term gives\n\\[\n2x+3=x+3,\n\\]\nso \\(x=0\\). Substituting gives the terms \\(-1,1,3\\), which do have common difference \\(2\\). Thus \\(x=0\\), choice B.",
